{
  "result": "At dawn, the city belongs to delivery trucks, insomniacs, and birds. Storefronts remain dark, traffic lights perform for empty intersections, and every sound seems briefly important. A bottle rolls along the curb like a small announcement. Somewhere, a bakery exhales warm air into an alley, turning the ordinary smell of yeast into an invitation.\n\nI like this hour because it reveals how much of daily life is prepared in secret. Floors are swept before shoes cross them. Coffee is brewed before anyone asks for it. Newspapers arrive folded around events that, only hours ago, felt unfinished. The world does not simply wake; it is assembled by hands we rarely notice.\n\nThere is comfort in that thought. We often imagine change as a trumpet blast: dramatic, public, impossible to ignore. Yet most lasting changes begin like morning preparations. A person reads ten pages each night. A neighbor learns another neighbor's name. Someone plants herbs in a chipped cup, saves a little money, apologizes without defending themselves, or walks one block farther than yesterday. No crowd applauds, but the street inside them gradually brightens.\n\nBy seven, buses sigh at corners and windows flash gold. The hidden workers disappear into the visible day, their efforts mistaken for the natural order of things. Perhaps that is the quiet lesson of dawn: beginnings need not be grand to be real. They need only be repeated, tended, and given enough time to meet the light. Then even the smallest habit can become a horizon worth following.",
